{
  "dom_id": "step-q311952",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "| **$x$** | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $0$ | $-12$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-4+3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
